Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 18:16:05 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] test: 146 cases against the phase 7 surface, most of them
 trying to break it
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

788 → 934, of which 393 → 446 run against a real Postgres.

The two adversarial files carry the weight. `phase7-onboarding-boundaries`
proves the lifecycle has no back door — and it runs as `authenticated`, the role
of the real path, not as `service_role`: column by column, `status`,
`submitted_at`, `submitted_by`, `template_id`, `project_id`, `client_id`. It
also covers each refusal written inside the three security-definer functions,
because there is no policy in the catalogue for a sweep to check.

`phase7-answer-integrity` proves the question spoof is refused for the
submission's own owner AND for `service_role`, which is what makes it an
invariant rather than an authorization rule.

Double submit gets its own case with the assertion that matters: the journey
advances at most once, and the log has exactly one line. Without the `for
update`, two taps would take the project from `onboarding` past `immersion` to
`research`, and the client would watch it happen.

The semantics of an answer exist in SQL and in TypeScript, so they are compared
against one shared table of 22 cases (`tests/support/answer-cases.ts`) — every
case run through both, one by one. Divergence is a red test instead of a
production surprise.

The component suite found a real race: clicking Submit blurs the field, so the
blur flush fires before the submit flush, and the second one saw an already
drained queue and concluded there was nothing pending. Fixed in the mechanism.

Source-code guards keep the mock dead, including through a careless revert.

